<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Guards (Finland)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-russia">Red Guards</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> were armed militias of workers formed by the labour movement in 1917, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> had removed the old order. In January 1918 they seized Helsinki and the industrial south, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Guards</a>. Poorly trained and led, they were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampere">Tampere</a> and elsewhere by May 1918, and thousands of their members died in executions and prison camps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-guards-finland.jpg" alt="Red Guards (Finland)" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julius Jääskeläinen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Guards_and_Russians_fighting_on_the_red_side_in_Ruovesi,_Pirkanmaa,_Finland,_1918._(50213249403).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 the Russian-controlled police lost its standing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and law enforcement passed to local labour organisations and then to a People's Militia, which the Senate disbanded after the Social Democrats lost their majority in the October 1917 election. Following a violent clash between striking farm workers and their employers at Huittinen on 13 July, right-wing farmers formed Protection Guards, and the labour movement answered with Workers' Order Guards. By the end of the general strike on 20 November they had 40,000–50,000 members under their first commander-in-chief, the newspaperman Ali Aaltonen.</p><p>On 6 January 1918 the Helsinki guard broke from the Social Democratic Party and took the name Helsinki Red Guard. After the Senate declared the Protection Corps to be government troops on 25 January, the guards merged into the Red Guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and revolution was proclaimed on the evening of 26 January with a red lantern on the tower of the Helsinki Workers' Hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The guards were made up of industrial workers, landless rural workers and crofters, mostly trade union members, aged on average between 20 and 30. A company was usually formed from one trade union local, such as the Helsinki shoemakers or tailors. Fighters were paid, sometimes more than in their usual work, which drew recruits at a time of high unemployment. Their strength rose from about 40,000 at the start of the war to 80,000–100,000 men and women, including 2,600 female fighters. Few had military training, officers were elected and discipline was lax, and most arms came from Russian troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Reds held a front from the Gulf of Bothnia to the Karelian Isthmus, but three offensives in February and March failed. After the Whites broke the front in northern Häme, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tampere">Battle of Tampere</a>, fought from 23 March to 6 April, ended with mass executions of surrendering Reds. German troops landed in the south and took Helsinki on 13 April, and more than 100,000 Red refugees headed east, of whom about 12,000 reached Soviet Russia. The Whites took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> on 29 April, and the last Reds surrendered to the Germans on 6 May.</p><p>About 80,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-russia">Red Guards</a> were taken prisoner, and 12,000 to 14,000 of them died in the prison camps of disease, malnutrition and execution. Most were pardoned in late 1918. Some of those who had fled fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>, and the Red Guards ceased to exist as an organised force by 1920.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Guards (Finland)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 28 January the <b>Red Guards</b> seized Helsinki, and the Finnish People's Delegation, chaired by Kullervo Manner, took power in the industrial south.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-socialist-workers-republic">Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic</a></li></ul></section>
